														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1446126">
									

<p>It needs some cleanup, but I <em>really</em> like the approach you're taking with space/time anomalies here. However, as a few people have noted, you have me somewhat confused in places. For example:</p>
<ul>
<li>Topology is pretty advanced stuff. To be honest I went into the article assuming that word was &quot;Topography&quot; before I looked it up.</li>
<li>The measure of &quot;w-axis deviations&quot; in &quot;degrees&quot; is somewhat ambiguous. It's not hard to guess that you're charting the 4th dimension on the W axis, but from there it gets fuzzy. My knee jerk reaction when shown the 4th dimension is, &quot;is this a time distortion&quot;? The more I look into some of the jargon, the less I'm sure. Also, since we're measuring in degrees, are you charting this via Polar, Spherical, or other non-cartesian coordinate system? I haven't done that sort of math in too long to remember what it would look like and what it would mean if you were. My initial assumptions were either that A) they're aging at a different rate from normal time, or B) they're just &quot;ahead&quot; or otherwise offset from normal time by a constant amount.</li>
<li>&quot;permitting objects with summed w-deviations of greater than 38 degrees to approach within 41 meters of a topological anomaly [causes shit to hit the fan]&quot; - this phrase is especially puzzling, given that EACH item is a topological anomaly. If I'm understanding the assertion correctly, if one of these statues had a 40 degree deviation, would it cause the singularity on its own?</li>
<li>How on earth do you have such a strong grasp of this field of mathematics if you have a law background???</li>
</ul>
<p>The information density is also kind of enormous. I think footnotes could do wonders to break things up here. For example, you could do something along the lines of &quot;Upon initial containment, the placement of of SCP-1272-01 through 04 in close proximity triggered a (some jargon phrase here) event<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-312819-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-312819-1')">1</a></sup>, resulting in a prolonged containment breach that is, as of yet, unresolved&quot;. In short, try to keep the core-body of the article more fluid for the lay-reader, and move the (admittedly fantastic) level of detail you've included into footnotes where they're more manageable and do less to detract from the core.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-312819-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-312819-1')">1</a>. Formation of a non-gravitational singularity caused when objects with summed w-deviations of greater than 38 degrees approach within 41 meters of a topological anomaly. This effect was unknown at time of containment</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
					
