			<div class="content" id="post-content-1446274">
									

<p>Well, except that usually when the term &quot;singularity&quot; is used to describe a phenomenon, it's generally assumed to refer to a <em>gravitational</em> singularity, such as one finds in a black hole. Technically, it refers to any mathematical or topological singularity, but that's not the way it's most often used.</p>
<p>The article is a bit thick; I didn't fully understand it until I got to the comments, because I'm only casually familiar with topography, but what I made out was quite intriguing. Could use some footnotes to explain things like what a <em>w</em>-axis is, and how many spatial dimensions are required to describe the phenomenon&#8230;</p>

